{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The auto-tagged ticket that was confidently wrong</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Your support tool uses a model to auto-tag incoming tickets by category, and applies the tag immediately so agents don't have to do it by hand. Most of the time it's right. Then a genuinely ambiguous ticket comes in, the model guesses a category it's barely sure about, and that guess gets applied exactly the same way as a confident, obvious one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in the UI told anyone the model was unsure. A 51%-confidence guess and a 99%-confidence guess look identical once they're both silently applied.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Every model call that drives an auto-applied UI action returns a confidence score alongside its output, most integrations just never read it. Treating every output as equally trustworthy throws away a signal the model is already computing for you.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This isn't a model-accuracy problem, it's a UI design problem. A model with 95% top-line accuracy is still wrong on the other 5%, and without a confidence gate, the UI has no way to tell those 5% apart from the 95% it should trust.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Auto-apply the confident output, surface the rest</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of applying every suggestion the same way, check the confidence score first. High-confidence suggestions apply automatically, same as before. Low-confidence ones go into a "needs review" state instead, so a human decides before anything actually changes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A confidence threshold turns one uniform behavior into two: <code>score >= threshold</code> auto-applies, anything below sits in a reviewable pending state with the suggestion visible but not yet committed. The threshold itself is a product decision, not a technical constant, tune it against how costly a wrong auto-apply actually is for that specific action.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The threshold should scale with the action's blast radius, not stay fixed across the whole product. Auto-tagging a support ticket at 70% confidence is low-stakes and easily corrected later; auto-applying a code suggestion or auto-sending a customer email at the same 70% is a much riskier default, and probably deserves a materially higher bar.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The auto-categorized refund that shouldn't have been</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's support tool auto-applied a "refund eligible" tag whenever the model detected a refund request, no confidence check. A sarcastic customer message ("oh great, ANOTHER broken order, refund me I guess") got tagged refund-eligible at low confidence, and the automated workflow behind that tag fired anyway.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody had reviewed it, because nothing in the system knew the model itself had been unsure. The tag looked exactly as certain as every other tag.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team added a confidence threshold specifically on the refund-eligible tag (a high-stakes action), leaving low-stakes tags like "billing question" auto-applying at a much lower bar. Ambiguous refund requests now sit in a review queue with the model's suggestion pre-filled, an agent confirms or overrides in seconds instead of the workflow firing blind.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also logged the confidence score alongside every auto-applied tag, not just the below-threshold ones, so a later audit could check whether the threshold itself was set correctly, catching threshold drift as the underlying model changed versions was something the original all-or-nothing design had no way to detect at all.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

